reason=Let an expedited incident PR omit its coverage and spec-enforcement members in ci.yml, under the maintainer ruling of 2026-11-05 ~21:12Z recorded on epic livespec-dev-tooling-bqnzcm (plan expedited-incident-response-flag). The `incident-ci` job gains one job output, `omitted`, written only after the independently installed RELEASED controller's `aggregate` has passed (it re-reads the observer's /current and /admitted answers) and projected from that aggregate's own JSON: the members it defers for the coverage or spec policy reason. The four existing jobs that hold those members (check-per-file-coverage, check-coverage, check-metadata, check-metadata-batch) now `needs:` incident-ci, carry a run-status-only job-level `if:` (`!cancelled()`) so that a failed or refused incident-ci leaves them running in full, guard the member's own steps on that output, and report an omitted member through an explicit `omitted-by-incident-policy` step; ci-green repeats the list. No label, repository variable or environment value is read to decide, a master push never reaches the step that writes the output, no job is added or removed, and ci-green requires the same eight jobs as before. Maintainer tier (label approval:workflow-edit): the edit modifies existing jobs and can reduce the checks an incident PR runs, which is exactly what the ruling authorizes. Runtime: the four jobs now start after incident-ci, which took 22 s (17 s queued) on run 42135733914 against a 29 min 39 s check-per-file-coverage; an incident PR runs less.
